{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'authority|IFRS|ASC|ASBE|revenue|lease|tax|foreign exchange|bond|subsidiary|consolidat' data/accounting-master/deliverables/P01 data/accounting-master/runs -g '*.json' -g '*.md' | head -260\"","aggregated_output":"rg: data/accounting-master/deliverables/P01: No such file or directory (os error 2)\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/controller-process-lifecycle/review_controller-0.response.json:9:    \"Inspected launch registration, deferred signals, unreaped leader ownership, normal cleanup, recovery signalling, descriptor ownership and lock release.\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/controller-process-lifecycle/review_controller-0.response.json:19:    \"P1 — Cleanup remains interruptible after registration. bounded_process restores the original signal mask before monitoring, and its finally block calls terminate_group without blocking SIGTERM/SIGINT. If an ordinary exception already initiated final cleanup, one handled signal during its sleeps or proc.wait raises KeyboardInterrupt and aborts cleanup. Similarly, another handled signal during cleanup after an initial interruption can leave live workers behind. The outer finally restores the mask but does not finish cleanup, and exclusive_lock subsequently releases the writer lock. stop_recovered_group has the same interruptible teardown pattern. Repair: defer handled termination throughout teardown, retain ownership and the lock until quiescence or a durably recorded unresolved-cleanup veto, then restore the original mask and propagate interruption. Add focused coverage for interruption during exception-path cleanup and repeated interruption during shutdown; the supplied registration-deferral test name does not establish these cases.\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/manifest.json:29:  \"stop_reason\": \"Controller review requires repair: process-lifecycle: P1 — Cleanup remains interruptible after registration. bounded_process restores the original signal mask before monitoring, and its finally block calls terminate_group without blocking SIGTERM/SIGINT. If an ordinary exception already initiated final cleanup, one handled signal during its sleeps or proc.wait raises KeyboardInterrupt and aborts cleanup. Similarly, another handled signal during cleanup after an initial interruption can leave live workers behind. The outer finally restores the mask but does not finish cleanup, and exclusive_lock subsequently releases the writer lock. stop_recovered_group has the same interruptible teardown pattern. Repair: defer handled termination throughout teardown, retain ownership and the lock until quiescence or a durably recorded unresolved-cleanup veto, then restore the original mask and propagate interruption. Add focused coverage for interruption during exception-path cleanup and repeated interruption during shutdown; the supplied registration-deferral test name does not establish these cases.; process-lifecycle: P2 — stop_recovered_group can leak an acquired pidfd on an identity-read exception. After open_pidfd succeeds, the descriptor is not added to descriptors until process_identity and identity validation finish. If that read raises, for example ProcessLookupError during process exit or an I/O error, the outer finally cannot close this descriptor. Repair: register descriptor ownership immediately after acquisition, or use a per-descriptor try/finally that closes it unless ownership transfers to descriptors. Handle disappearance consistently as a vanished process while preserving fail-closed treatment of other identity-read failures. Verify that every successfully opened descriptor closes on validation failure and exception.; permissions-install: Invalid worker list: issues; worker-boundary: WB-1 — Response freshness is not enforced. In Master._worker_result, destination is folder / f'{role}-{attempt}.response.json'. An existing file is neither rejected nor distinguished from output of the current invocation. If that pathname is reused and the current child exits successfully without replacing it, destination.exists() succeeds and an old PLAN, AGREE or IMPLEMENTED response passes validation. The subsequent atomic_json rewrite makes the old response look like a newly persisted result. This is a stale-acceptance path unless callers guarantee fresh output paths, an interface invariant absent from the packet. Repair: allocate a fresh invocation-specific output pathname, reject unexpected pre-existing output, and consume only that invocation's validated response. Preserve earlier responses as evidence rather than deleting or overwriting them. If uniqueness is supplied by callers, document and enforce it at this boundary.; worker-boundary: WB-2 — Cleanup failure short-circuits integrity verification. In Master.process.finally, remaining group members cause ContinuationVeto before verify_hashes executes; failure to publish active-process.json also exits before that check. The outer worker finally provides partial coverage, but it does not explicitly include the process-local next-plan.json snapshot. Thus the supplied code does not implement the stated integrity-check coverage across all child failure paths. It already blocks continuation on survivors, so this finding is about missing integrity evidence and guard coverage, not permission to launch another writer. Repair: structure cleanup/status publication and integrity verification as independently attempted finalization operations, retain both failures and any available process result, and raise a continuation veto after collecting them. With live survivors, label the integrity snapshot provisional and require cleanup plus another integrity check before resume.; state-refresh: Blocked phases can stay blocked after the condition that blocked them changes. refresh() skips blocked_external when its stored signature matches input_fingerprint(), but that fingerprint excludes the phase's external inputs or availability. Include the specific inputs relevant to each block in the signature, or make the block explicitly retryable when they cannot be fingerprinted; test a changed external condition on resume.; state-refresh: A crash during refresh() can leave a partial next-plan.md and a next-plan.json that describes phase states not yet saved to state.json. Publish the refreshed state and plan as one recoverable generation, or add a recovery protocol that detects and rebuilds mismatched plan files before they are used; write Markdown atomically.; scheduler: Fresh reviewed plans are not enforced for subsequent phases. `controller_review(folder)` is called only once before the loop, while each later `self.refresh()` result is used immediately to select and execute the next phase. A repair or phase result can therefore alter eligibility or next actions without a new review/approval. Repair by attaching a reviewed-plan identity/hash to each refreshed plan and requiring a successful read-only review before every subsequent phase, including after repair-triggered refreshes; reject execution when that review is absent or stale.; scheduler: Protection is silently disabled when `data/financial-report-benchmark/release-manifest.json` is absent: `self.protected` becomes `{}`, after which `check_protection()` can perform no frozen-evidence comparison. This permits a run to proceed without the stated source/release/research/snapshot and controller-hash protection. Treat a missing or malformed release manifest as a continuation veto (or otherwise require an explicitly recorded, reviewed protection scope) and persist that failure in the run manifest.; scheduler: A pre-existing stop request is renamed to `prior-stop-request.json` before the scheduler loop, so `not stop.exists()` is true and the invocation proceeds. This can resume work despite an outstanding ordinary interruption request. Preserve the request as evidence but set the run outcome to `stop_requested` before phase execution (or leave an active stop marker until an explicit operator action clears it).; phase-plan-repair: scripts/run_accounting_master.py — Master.execute_phase resets the deletion baseline before every repair. If implementation deletes a pre-existing tracked file, changes_since detects it and raises MasterError. The next repair snapshots the already-damaged tree, so leaving that file absent no longer appears in changes['deleted']. Passing checks and AGREE can then accept the phase without restoring the prohibited deletion. A worker returning BLOCKED bypasses changes_since entirely, creating the same risk on resume. Preserve a durable phase-entry inventory and byte references across retries and resumed attempts; reconcile deletions after every editable worker outcome, including BLOCKED and handled failures. Require restoration of every outstanding prohibited deletion before acceptance. Keep cumulative changes available to the reviewer as well as the latest repair delta.; phase-plan-repair: scripts/run_accounting_master.py — Master.execute_phase handles BLOCKED only from plan and implementation/repair workers. A BLOCKED plan review is treated as a request for plan repair; a BLOCKED result review triggers another editable repair. Exhaustion becomes needs_plan_repair or needs_repair rather than blocked_external, defeating the stated rule that unchanged external blocks are skipped and potentially repeating work against an unavailable prerequisite. Handle BLOCKED explicitly after both review calls, preserve its issues and residuals, and use the existing external-block tracking path. If review roles cannot emit BLOCKED, that missing interface restriction must be stated and an explicit supported mechanism must convey external blockers discovered during review.; evidence: Preservation is not interruption-safe. preserve_editable writes directly to the final content-addressed destination with write_bytes. An ordinary interruption or write failure can leave a truncated file under the correct digest name. On resume, that file exists, so the function raises 'Preserved pre-edit content is corrupt' rather than recovering, even if the original bytes remain available. New copies are also not verified or fsynced before success is returned. Repair: write to a temporary sibling, verify its digest against the inventoried value, flush/fsync, atomically publish and fsync the directory. Recover an incomplete existing object only from bytes verified against the expected digest; otherwise stop while retaining evidence.; evidence: Hash-map composition silently discards conflicting protection requirements. release_hashes overwrites release-manifest entries with integrity-inventory entries; protected_hashes can overwrite earlier expected hashes with current filesystem hashes; check_protection merges controls over protected hashes. If overlapping entries disagree, only the last expectation is checked, so a protection check can pass despite violating an earlier frozen expectation. Repair: merge through a helper that permits duplicate paths only when expected hashes agree, rejects conflicts before execution, and never replaces an authoritative expected hash with a freshly observed hash.; evidence: Legacy-check failure logs are lost on the repair path. baseline captures stdout in StringIO but writes the log only after runpy.run_path returns normally. If a check raises or exits unsuccessfully, the captured diagnostic output disappears; stderr is not included either. This removes evidence needed to explain the failure and inform repair and subsequent review. Repair: persist captured output in a finally block, capture stderr as well, record the failure outcome, and propagate the original failure without converting it into acceptance.\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T154913-29ec15cd/controller-process-permissions/review_controller-0.response.json:18:    \"HIGH — Standalone review does not handle SIGTERM safely. main executes controller_review outside catch_termination, although that review can launch a worker through process. With the normal SIGTERM disposition, the supervisor exits without Python finally cleanup; the child runs in a separate session and can survive while the supervisor's flock is released. Extend termination handling over every child-launching action, including review, and retain durable cleanup-required status until descendant termination is verified.\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/controller-scheduler/review_controller-0.response.json:5:    \"Frozen-evidence protection is required whenever the controller runs; absence of the release manifest is not an authorization to disable it.\"\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/controller-scheduler/review_controller-0.response.json:18:    \"Protection is silently disabled when `data/financial-report-benchmark/release-manifest.json` is absent: `self.protected` becomes `{}`, after which `check_protection()` can perform no frozen-evidence comparison. This permits a run to proceed without the stated source/release/research/snapshot and controller-hash protection. Treat a missing or malformed release manifest as a continuation veto (or otherwise require an explicitly recorded, reviewed protection scope) and persist that failure in the run manifest.\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/20261002T164110-7313e7d0/controller-evidence/review_controller-0.response.json:19:    \"Hash-map composition silently discards conflicting protection requirements. release_hashes overwrites release-manifest entries with integrity-inventory entries; protected_hashes can overwrite earlier expected hashes with current filesystem hashes; check_protection merges controls over protected hashes. If overlapping entries disagree, only the last expectation is checked, so a protection check can pass despite violating an earlier frozen expectation. Repair: merge through a helper that permit