 represents total net revenue accordance with U.S. GAAP and this presentation is referred less total noninterest expense to as “reported” basis; these financial statements appear on • Net interest income and net yield excluding CIB Mar\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/preflight-repair-20261002/P00/corpus-integrity.json:5909:          \"quote\": \"-GAAP FINANCIAL MEASURES The Firm prepares its Consolidated Financial Statements in • Pre-provision profit, which represents total net revenue accordance with U.S. GAAP and this presentation is referred less total noninterest expense to as “reported” basis; these financial statements appear on • Net interest income and net yield excluding CIB Mar\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/preflight-repair-20261002/P00/corpus-integrity.json:5924:          \"quote\": \"-GAAP FINANCIAL MEASURES The Firm prepares its Consolidated Financial Statements in • Pre-provision profit, which represents total net revenue accordance with U.S. GAAP and this presentation is referred less total noninterest expense to as “reported” basis; these financial statements appear on • Net interest income and net yield excluding CIB Mar\",\ndata/accounting-master/runs/preflight-repair-20261002/P00/corpus-integrity.json:5939:          \"quote\": \"-GAAP financial measures The Firm prepares its Consolidated Financial Statements in management to assess the comparability of revenue from accordance with U.S. GAAP; these financial statements year-to-year arising from both taxable and tax-exempt appear on pages 159-163. That presentation, which is sources. The corresponding income tax impact\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers -maxdepth 1 -type f -print -exec sed -n '1,180p' {} \\\\;\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers -maxdepth 1 -type f -print -exec sed -n '1,180p' {} \\\\;\"","aggregated_output":"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/inventory.md\n# Inventory: costing and reversal\n\nStatus: internally checked illustration; no paid review requested. Related\nrequirements MC07, MC19, MC20; gaps G4, G7, G12.\n\n## Proposed treatment and checked consequence\n\nFor interchangeable goods outside the special scope exclusions, apply one\nconsistent FIFO or weighted-average formula to inventories of similar nature\nand use under the inspected IAS-equivalent text. The same purchases (10 units\nat 5, then 10 at 7) and sale of 12 give:\n\n| Formula | Cost of sales | Closing stock |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| FIFO | 64 | 56 |\n| LIFO illustration/company-policy profile | 80 | 40 |\n| Periodic weighted average | 72 | 48 |\n\nAll three account for the same total cost 120. Their differing closing values\ncannot be removed by relabelling concepts. Under the inspected IAS-equivalent\nreversal rule, cost 56 and net realisable value 50 produce an expense of 6.\nIf the original conditions reverse and NRV rises to 54, restore 4. If NRV rises\nto 60, restore only 6, leaving carrying value 56. The original cost and prior\nwrite-down must therefore remain in state.\n\nThe [calculation report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) checks these amounts\nand the cap. It does not decide whether an actual inventory population is\ninterchangeable or whether a particular NRV estimate is justified.\n\n## Inspected sources\n\n| Source and exact local anchor | What it supplies | Applicability limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [AASB 102](../../research/standards/aasb102.pdf), PDF pp. 4–5, 8–10; scope 2–5, formulas 23–27, NRV 28–34, effective date 40 | IAS 2 relationship; specific identification versus FIFO/average; consistent use; reversal capped at original write-down | July 2015 issue, effective for annual periods starting on/after 1 January 2018 with stated early application. Later amendments not checked here; Australian-specific provisions and Tier 2 must not be silently treated as IFRS equivalence. |\n| [Caterpillar 2024 report](../../research/standards/caterpillar2024.pdf), Note 1.C (PDF p. 61) and Note 8 (PDF p. 78) | Company says approximately 65% of inventory uses LIFO; FIFO comparison; reported inventory 16,827 million | Company-policy evidence, not the text of ASC 330. |\n| [ASBE 1 reproduction](../../research/standards/asbe-reproduction.html), Articles 14–19 | Attributed costing, lower-of-cost/NRV and reversal wording | Unofficial reproduction; current official version and amendments remain unverified. |\n\nFile hashes, original URLs and acquisition limits are retained in\n[standards support](../../research/standards-support.json).\n\n## Strongest alternative interpretation\n\nSpecific project items may require specific identification. Commodity\nbroker-traders and other scope exceptions change the measurement question.\nGrouped write-downs require their own support; an operating-segment total is\nnot sufficient. US LIFO measurement cannot be inferred from an IAS reversal\nexample or one issuer's abbreviated policy. The proposed answer is conditional\non the stated population, jurisdiction, period and source authority.\n\n## Internal preparation before a possible professional question\n\nAcquire the applicable primary ASC/ASBE text through legitimate access and\ncheck amendment dates; collect actual inventory policies, lot/quantity data,\npopulation definitions, NRV evidence and previous impairments. Reconcile a\ncompany's specific-identification/FIFO/average/LIFO population and reproduce\nits disclosed reserve before proposing a treatment. These are internal tasks.\n\nOnly then could a narrow question be: given the attached population and evidence,\ndoes paragraph X permit the proposed cost formula, grouping or reversal? Supply\nthe proposed journal, the competing reading and their numerical difference.\nIf no ambiguity remains, no professional answer is needed for this illustration.\ndocs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/compound-bond.md\n# Compound bond: issuer classification and settlement\n\nStatus: internally checked conditional illustration; actual instrument\nclassification is not established. MC17, MC19; gaps G4 and G7.\n\n## Facts and proposed answer\n\nThe issuer receives 100, owes two annual coupons of 5 and principal 100, and\nthe holder may convert that principal into ten ordinary shares. Currency is\nthe issuer's functional currency. Assume no fees, other options, contingencies\nor settlement alternatives; assume the share-conversion feature qualifies as\nequity. The market yield for otherwise comparable non-convertible debt is\nassumed to be 10%, not estimated from the example.\n\nUnder those conditions the proposed issuer split is liability\n`5/1.1 + 105/1.1² = 11050/121`, and residual equity `1050/121`.\nThe exact calculation keeps the components summing to 100. Using the stated\neffective-interest schedule, liability after coupons one and two is `1050/11`\nand 100. Interest expense totals `2260/121`, including discount accretion.\n\nIssue: debit cash 100; credit liability `11050/121` and conversion equity\n`1050/121`. Accrual debits interest expense and credits liability; each coupon\ndebits liability and credits cash 5. At maturity, redemption debits liability\nand credits cash 100. Conversion instead debits liability and credits issued\nshare equity 100; the original equity component remains in equity. The\n[checked journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) preserves both\nsettlement paths. A 5% comparable yield gives liability 100 as an arithmetic\nboundary case; it is not evidence that a real conversion option has zero value.\n\n## Source and boundary\n\n[AASB 132](../../research/standards/aasb132.pdf), pp. 12–14 and 25–26:\nparagraph 22 describes fixed-number/fixed-amount settlement; 25–27 address\ncontingencies and settlement choices; 28–32 require the compound split and\nresidual; AG30 confines this treatment to the issuer, AG31 supplies comparable\nliability discounting, AG32 supplies no maturity-conversion gain/loss, and\nAG33–AG35 distinguish early repurchase and induced conversion. Paragraph 31\nrefers subsequent financial-liability measurement to AASB 9. This dossier's\naccretion calculation is a declared schedule; it does not fill the missing\nprimary measurement-source audit by implication.\n\nThe PDF's pp. 1 and 5 say the compilation applies to annual periods beginning\non/after 1 January 2023 and **before 1 July 2026**, incorporating amendments\nthrough 15 December 2022. Period start determines that condition; a calendar\n2026 annual period is inside it. The local source is an official Australian\nIAS-equivalent text with the comparison qualifications on p. 4, not proof of\nevery jurisdiction's currently adopted version.\n\n## Strongest counterargument and remaining internal work\n\nA variable share count, cash option, foreign-currency exercise amount, put,\nreset clause, contingent payment or related guarantee could defeat the assumed\nclassification. The arithmetic can remain perfectly balanced while the\nclassification is wrong. Do not apply this issuer example to the holder.\n\nFor an actual bond, first retain the complete contract and side agreements,\nprepare a clause-by-clause settlement table, establish reporting basis and\nperiod, acquire the applicable primary measurement authority, document the\ncomparable yield and test early settlement/modification where relevant.\nUS-GAAP and ASBE classification need their own dated sources. All are internal\npreparation tasks; do not pay for a reviewer to discover the missing clauses.\n\nA later review can ask whether named clauses X/Y change the proposed equity\nclassification under a cited paragraph, with both proposed treatments and\ntheir reconciled entries supplied. No actual clause requiring that judgment\nhas been supplied in the current synthetic example.\ndocs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/insurance.md\n# Short-duration insurance and separate reinsurance\n\nStatus: internally checked PAA illustration, not a general IFRS 17 engine or\nan actuarial estimate. MC19, MC22; gaps G7 and G16.\n\n## Conditions that make the example work\n\nBoth the issued contracts and the proportionate reinsurance treaty have\none-year coverage. Underlying policies and treaty start together and are\nrecognised then. Both service patterns are uniform; there are no acquisition\ncash flows, investment components or significant financing effects. Remaining\ngross coverage is assumed non-onerous. Claim and recovery cash flows are\nexpected within one year of the underlying claims being incurred. Gross risk\nadjustment 2 and transferred-risk adjustment 1 are separately supplied inputs;\nzero expected reinsurer non-performance effect is another explicit assumption.\n\nThe manuscript now states separate treaty eligibility and recognition timing.\nShort underlying policies alone would not establish that a longer or differently\ntimed reinsurance contract qualifies for the same simplification.\n\n## Proposed accounting and exact reconciliation\n\nCapital 20 plus received premium 120, less ceded premium 24 and claims paid 30,\nplus recovery received 15 gives cash 101. Half of gross service releases 60\nfrom remaining coverage into revenue; half of ceded service releases 12 from\nthe reinsurance coverage asset into expense. Gross incurred claims of 40 plus\nrisk adjustment 2 give expense 42 and, after payment 30, liability 12. Treaty\nrecoveries of 20 plus transferred-risk adjustment 1 give recovery income 21\nand, after receipts 15, recovery asset 6.\n\n| Reconciliation | Amount |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Assets: cash 101 + reinsurance coverage 12 + claims recovery 6 | 119 |\n| Liabilities: gross remaining coverage 60 + incurred claims 12 | 72 |\n| Profit: revenue 60 − gross claims 42 − ceded service 12 + recovery 21 | 27 |\n| Equity: opening capital 20 + profit 27 | 47 |\n\nThe [journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) checks every operation's\nbalance and the final identity. Changing transferred risk to 1.5, holding other\ninputs fixed, changes the recovery asset to 6.5 and profit to 27.5. An illustrative\nexpected non-performance reduction of 2 instead produces recovery asset 4 and\nprofit 25. Those are conditional calculation checks, not estimated risks. They\nshow why a blanket “halve all gross balances” operation is wrong for this model.\nGross and ceded portfolio balances remain separate; the coverage/claims split\nis an internal roll-forward, not an assertion that each is a required face line.\n\n## Inspected authority and strongest objections\n\n[AASB 17](../../research/standards/aasb17.pdf): pp. 15–18, paragraphs 53–59,\n60–70A, establish PAA eligibility/movement, short cash-flow discounting relief,\nseparate reinsurance recognition and eligibility, non-performance and transferred\nrisk. Paragraphs 78–82 (pp. 19–20) distinguish issued and held portfolios and\ntheir performance. B126–B127 (p. 54) use passage of time unless expected risk\nrelease differs significantly and require reconsideration when facts change.\nParagraphs 62–62A matter to when the treaty first enters the example; 69(b)\nmatters separately from 53(b).\n\nThis official Australian compilation applies to annual periods beginning\non/after 1 January 2023 and before 1 July 2026, with amendments through\n15 December 2022 (PDF pp. 1, 5). It is inspected IASB-equivalent paragraph\nsupport within the stated jurisdiction/version limits, not blanket current\nIFRS compliance. [Source hashes](../../research/standards-support.json) bind\nthe version read.\n\nThe strongest objection is that one or more assumed eligibility, service,\ncash-flow timing or risk inputs may fail for a real group. A multi-year treaty,\nsignificant differences in risk release, onerous underlying contracts, treaty\nlimits/disputes or longer claim settlement changes the accounting work needed.\nA chain-ladder point projection does not supply the risk adjustment or an\nentire predictive law.\n\n## Work before referral\n\nInternally assemble dated policies/treaty terms, grouping/recognition dates,\ncoverage boundaries, expected service/claims/recovery timing, onerous-group\nassessment, non-performance evidence and actuarial input methods. Extend the\noperations and uncertainty checks where the example's assumptions fail.\nReconcile gross and ceded movements separately before requesting a judgment.\n\nOnly a remaining contract-specific issue should become a question: does the\nattached treaty qualify under paragraph 69, or does clause X/its cash-flow\nevidence change recognition, allocation or measurement? Supply the proposed\nanswer and competing calculation. This packet is not ready for paid review of\nan actual insurer because those actual facts have not been supplied.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
