venue and receivable; reduce tax expense 2 into tax prepayment; corrected profit 6\"\ndocs/design/fixtures/northstar.json:576:      \"content\": \"Financing note applies to debt and interest in every statement\"\ndocs/design/fixtures/temporal.json:6:      \"key\": \"revenue:H1:USD:NS:Demo\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/temporal.json:15:      \"key\": \"revenue:H1:USD:NS:Demo\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/temporal.json:26:      \"key\": \"revenue:FY:USD:NS:Demo\",\ndocs/design/research/source-support.json:1334:      \"title\": \"A Dynamic Microeconometric Simulation Model for Incorporated Businesses\",\ndocs/design/research/source-support.json:1340:      \"local_path\": \"docs/papers/A Dynamic Microeconometric Simulation Model for Incorporated Businesses Shahnazarian (2004).pdf\",\ndocs/design/research/source-support.json:1341:      \"original_path\": \"docs/ML for accounting and management/A Dynamic Microeconometric Simulation Model for Incorporated Businesses Shahnazarian(04).pdf\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:21:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:43:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:63:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:83:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:103:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:125:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:147:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:169:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:191:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:211:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:233:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:253:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:275:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:295:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:319:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/research/tools.json:343:      \"version_status\": \"content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:6:      \"id\": \"synthetic_corporate\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:11:        \"all corporate example statements\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:18:      \"id\": \"us_corporate\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:39:      \"id\": \"ifrs_corporate\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:44:        \"revenue/working capital\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:49:      \"id\": \"asbe_corporate\",\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:102:        \"comparative revisions and consolidation\"\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:113:  \"release_criteria\": {\ndocs/design/fixtures/corpus-protocol.json:114:    \"critical_errors\": \"zero source-loss, entity/unit/time/basis errors, invented facts, hidden financing, silently lost branches or unreported unsupported operations in the declared conformance corpus\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:241:      \"reason\": \"Neural adsorption models concern chemical mechanisms rather than corporate records or financial forecasting methods adopted here.\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:384:      \"reason\": \"Carbon-information continuity raises adjacent disclosure/provenance questions; sustainability measurement is not formalised in this corporate reference package.\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:538:      \"reason\": \"DeFi is relevant if tokenised instruments are added; the reference corporate/bank instruments do not execute decentralised protocols.\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:681:      \"reason\": \"QA dataset taxonomy could inform later corpus selection; the present release corpus is specified directly from filing tasks.\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:802:      \"reason\": \"Criticality-aware synthetic ESG tables could inform error severity annotation; the design's accounting release criteria need domain-specific adjudication.\",\ndocs/design/research/forward-screening-decisions.json:901:      \"reason\": \"IoT prompting is not a financial table or whole-report extraction comparator for the declared release.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1293:    \"parent_tag\": \"us-gaap:revenueremainingperformanceobligationexpectedtimingofsatisfactionstartdateaxis.domain\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1314:    \"parent_tag\": \"us-gaap:revenueremainingperformanceobligationexpectedtimingofsatisfactionstartdateaxis.domain\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1335:    \"parent_tag\": \"us-gaap:revenueremainingperformanceobligationexpectedtimingofsatisfactionstartdateaxis.domain\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1356:    \"parent_tag\": \"us-gaap:revenueremainingperformanceobligationexpectedtimingofsatisfactionstartdateaxis.domain\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1621:    \"text\": \"us-gaap:CorporateDebtSecuritiesMember\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:1964:    \"text\": \"us-gaap:CorporateDebtSecuritiesMember\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:3322:    \"text\": \"us-gaap:CorporateNonSegmentMember\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:3350:    \"text\": \"us-gaap:CorporateNonSegmentMember\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:4421:    \"text\": \"CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:4785:    \"text\": \"Income before provision for income taxes\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:4806:    \"text\": \"Provision for income taxes\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:4988:    \"text\": \"See accompanying Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:5009:    \"text\": \"CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF COMPREHENSIVE INCOME (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:5401:    \"text\": \"See accompanying Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:5422:    \"text\": \"CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:5835:    \"text\": \"Deferred revenue\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:6255:    \"text\": \"See accompanying Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:6276:    \"text\": \"CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF SHAREHOLDERS’ EQUITY (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:6934:    \"text\": \"See accompanying Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:6955:    \"text\": \"CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF CASH FLOWS (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:7620:    \"text\": \"Financing activities:\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:7907:    \"text\": \"Cash used in financing activities\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8019:    \"text\": \"Cash paid for income taxes, net\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8054:    \"text\": \"See accompanying Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8075:    \"text\": \"Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements (Unaudited)\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8103:    \"text\": \"The condensed consolidated financial statements include the accounts of Apple Inc. and its wholly owned subsidiaries (collectively “Apple” or the “Company”). In the opinion of the Company’s management, the condensed consolidated financial statements reflect all adjustments, which are normal and recurring in nature, necessary for fair financial statement presentation. The preparation of these condensed consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes in conformity with U.S. generally accepted accounting principles (“GAAP”) requires the use of management estimates. These condensed consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes should be read in conjunction with the Company’s annual consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes included in its Annual Report on Form 10-K for the fiscal year ended September 28, 2024 (the “2024 Form 10-K”).\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8124:    \"text\": \"Revenue\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8362:    \"text\": \" billion of revenue recognized in the three months ended December 28, 2024 that was included in deferred revenue as of September 28, 2024 and $\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8376:    \"text\": \" billion of revenue recognized in the three months ended December 30, 2023 that was included in deferred revenue as of September 30, 2023.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8383:    \"text\": \"The Company’s proportion of net sales by disaggregated revenue source was generally consistent for each reportable segment in Note 10, “Segment Information and Geographic Data” for the three months ended December 28, 2024 and December 30, 2023, except in Greater China, where iPhone revenue represented a moderately higher proportion of net sales.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8390:    \"text\": \"As of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024, the Company had total deferred revenue of $\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:8432:    \"text\": \"% of total deferred revenue to be realized in less than a year, \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:9559:    \"text\": \"Corporate debt securities\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:10749:    \"text\": \"Corporate debt securities\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11351:    \"text\": \"The Company may use derivative instruments to partially offset its business exposure to foreign exchange and interest rate risk. However, the Company may choose not to hedge certain exposures for a variety of reasons, including accounting considerations or the prohibitive economic cost of hedging particular exposures. There can be no assurance the hedges will offset more than a portion of the financial impact resulting from movements in foreign exchange or interest rates.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11358:    \"text\": \"Foreign Exchange Rate Risk\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11365:    \"text\": \"To protect gross margins from fluctuations in foreign exchange rates, the Company may use forwards, options or other instruments, and may designate these instruments as cash flow hedges. The Company generally hedges portions of its forecasted foreign currency exposure associated with revenue and inventory purchases, typically for up to \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11386:    \"text\": \"To protect the Company’s foreign currency–denominated term debt or marketable securities from fluctuations in foreign exchange rates, the Company may use forwards, cross-currency swaps or other instruments. The Company designates these instruments as either cash flow or fair value hedges. As of December 28, 2024, the maximum length of time over which the Company is hedging its exposure to the variability in future cash flows for term debt–related foreign currency transactions is \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11407:    \"text\": \"The Company may also use derivative instruments that are not designated as accounting hedges to protect gross margins from certain fluctuations in foreign exchange rates, as well as to offset a portion of the foreign currency gains and losses generated by the remeasurement of certain assets and liabilities denominated in non-functional currencies.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11477:    \"text\": \"Foreign exchange contracts\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11554:    \"text\": \"Foreign exchange contracts\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11848:    \"text\": \"Condensed Consolidated Financial Statement Details\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:11855:    \"text\": \"The following tables show the Company’s condensed consolidated financial statement details as of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024 (in millions):\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:12170:    \"text\": \"The Company issues unsecured short-term promissory notes pursuant to a commercial paper program. The Company uses net proceeds from the commercial paper program for general corporate purposes, including dividends and share repurchases. As of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024, the Company had $\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:12632:    \"text\": \"The following table shows share-based compensation expense and the related income tax benefit included in the Condensed Consolidated Statements of Operations for the three months ended December 28, 2024 and December 30, 2023 (in millions):\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:12709:    \"text\": \"Income tax benefit related to share-based compensation expense\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:13276:    \"text\": \"A reconciliation of the Company’s segment operating income to the Condensed Consolidated Statements of Operations for the three months ended December 28, 2024 and December 30, 2023 is as follows (in millions):\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:13402:    \"text\": \"Other corporate expenses, net\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:13528:    \"text\": \"The following discussion should be read in conjunction with the 2024 Form 10-K filed with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (the “SEC”) and the condensed consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes included in Part I, Item 1 of this Form 10-Q.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:13542:    \"text\": \"The Company periodically provides certain information for investors on its corporate website, www.apple.com, and its investor relations website, investor.apple.com. This includes press releases and other information about financial performance, information on environmental, social and governance matters, and details related to the Company’s annual meeting of shareholders. The information contained on the websites referenced in this Form 10-Q is not incorporated by reference into this filing. Further, the Company’s references to website URLs are intended to be inactive textual references only.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15040:    \"text\": \"Provision for Income Taxes\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15047:    \"text\": \"Provision for income taxes, effective tax rate and statutory federal income tax rate for the three months ended December 28, 2024 and December 30, 2023 were as follows (dollars in millions):\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15089:    \"text\": \"Provision for income taxes\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15159:    \"text\": \"Statutory federal income tax rate\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15194:    \"text\": \"The Company’s effective tax rate for the first quarter of 2025 was lower than the statutory federal income tax rate due primarily to a lower effective tax rate on foreign earnings, tax benefits from share-based compensation, the impact of foreign currency loss regulations issued by the U.S. Department of the Treasury in December 2024, and the tax impact from foreign currency revaluations related to the State Aid Decision.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15341:    \"text\": \", which clarified the effective date of ASU 2024-03. ASU 2024-03 will require the Company to disclose the amounts of purchases of inventory, employee compensation, depreciation and intangible asset amortization, as applicable, included in certain expense captions in the Consolidated Statements of Operations, as well as qualitatively describe remaining amounts included in those captions. ASU 2024-03 will also require the Company to disclose both the amount and the Company’s definition of selling expenses. The Company will adopt ASU 2024-03 in its fourth quarter of 2028 using a prospective transition method.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15348:    \"text\": \"Income Taxes\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15362:    \"text\": \"Income Taxes (Topic 740): Improvements to Income Tax Disclosures \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15369:    \"text\": \"(“ASU 2023-09”), which will require the Company to disclose specified additional information in its income tax rate reconciliation and provide additional information for reconciling items that meet a quantitative threshold. ASU 2023-09 will also require the Company to disaggregate its income taxes paid disclosure by federal, state and foreign taxes, with further disaggregation required for significant individual jurisdictions. The Company will adopt ASU 2023-09 in its fourth quarter of 2026 using a prospective transition method.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15411:    \"text\": \"The preparation of financial statements and related disclosures in conformity with GAAP and the Company’s discussion and analysis of its financial condition and operating results require the Company’s management to make judgments, assumptions and estimates that affect the amounts reported. Note 1, “Summary of Significant Accounting Policies” of the Notes to Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements in Part I, Item 1 of this Form 10-Q and in the Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements in Part II, Item 8 of the 2024 Form 10-K describe the significant accounting policies and methods used in the preparation of the Company’s condensed consolidated financial statements. There have been no material changes to the Company’s critical accounting estimates since the 2024 Form 10-K.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15600:    \"text\": \"The Company’s global operations are subject to complex and changing laws and regulations on subjects including antitrust; privacy, data security and data localization; consumer protection; advertising, sales, billing and e-commerce; financial services and technology; product liability; intellectual property ownership and infringement; digital platforms; machine learning and artificial intelligence; internet, telecommunications and mobile communications; media, television, film and digital content; availability of third-party software applications and services; labor and employment; anticorruption; import, export and trade; foreign exchange controls and cash repatriation restrictions; anti–money laundering; foreign ownership and investment; national security; tax; and environmental, health and safety, including electronic waste, recycling, product design and climate change.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:15663:    \"text\": \"Further, the Company has commercial relationships with other companies in the technology industry that are or may become subject to investigations and litigation that, if resolved against those other companies, could materially adversely affect the Company’s commercial relationships with those business partners and materially adversely affect the Company’s business, results of operations and financial condition. For example, the Company earns revenue from licensing arrangements with Google LLC (“Google”) and other companies to offer their search services on the Company’s platforms and applications, and certain of these arrangements are currently subject to government investigations and legal proceedings. On August 5, 2024, Google was found to have violated U.S. antitrust laws. In connection with this finding, the DOJ has proposed remedies, which include changes to Google’s products, services and business models. The proposed changes vary in scope and severity and range from imposing significant restrictions on Google’s licensing agreements to prohibiting Google from offering the Company commercial terms for search distribution for up to 10 years. If implemented, such remedies could materially adversely affect the Company’s ability to earn revenue from such licensing arrangements.\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:16034:    \"text\": \"former Senior Vice President and Chief Financial Officer, and current Vice President, Corporate Services\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:16055:    \"text\": \" into a trading plan intended to satisfy the affirmative defense conditions of Rule 10b5-1(c) under the Exchange Act. The plan provides for the sale, subject to certain price limits, of shares vesting between April 1, 2025 and October 15, 2025, pursuant to certain equity awards granted to Mr. Maestri, excluding any shares withheld by the Company to satisfy income tax withholding and remittance obligations. Mr. Maestri’s plan will \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:16146:    \"text\": \" into a trading plan intended to satisfy the affirmative defense conditions of Rule 10b5-1(c) under the Exchange Act. The plan provides for the sale of shares vesting between April 1, 2025 and October 1, 2025, pursuant to certain equity awards granted to Ms. Adams, excluding any shares withheld by the Company to satisfy income tax withholding and remittance obligations. Ms. Adam’s plan will expire on \",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:16174:    \"text\": \"Incorporated by Reference\",\ndocs/design/cases/apple/interim-source-units.json:16342:    \"text\": \"Inline XBRL Document Set for the condensed consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes in Part I, Item 1, “Financial Statements” of this Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q.\",\ndocs/design/research/software/ledger_ledger-meta.json:66:  \"releases_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/ledger/ledger/releases{/id}\",\ndocs/design/research/software/docling-project_docling-meta.json:66:  \"releases_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/docling-project/docling/releases{/id}\",\ndocs/design/research/software/microsoft_table-transformer-meta.json:66:  \"releases_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/microsoft/table-transformer/releases{/id}\",\ndocs/design/review/pre-repair-2026-10-02/scripts/build_design_research.py:63: 'merigoux2021': [('Reiter (1980), A Logic for Default Reasoning','FOUNDATIONAL','Catala paper inspected for its restricted calculus; no general default-logic theorem imported'),('Merigoux et al., reverse engineering French income tax','DIRECT_METHOD','tax-language context; not corporate accounting evidence')],\ndocs/design/review/pre-repair-2026-10-02/scripts/build_design_research.py:87: 'shahnazarian2004': [('Dynamic corporate microsimulation and tax-response models in Chapter 1','DIRECT_METHOD','historical context; do not import calibration defaults')],\ndocs/design/review/pre-repair-2026-10-02/scripts/build_design_research.py:297:        records.append({'repository':r['full_name'],'url':r['html_url'],'default_branch_at_access':r['default_branch'],'access_date':DATE,'last_push_metadata':r['pushed_at'],'archived':r['archived'],'github_license_detection':(r.get('license') or {}).get('spdx_id','not available'),'local_files':files,'sha256':hashes,'inspection_level':'official description/license and selected interfaces where archived; no integration executed','version_status':'content snapshots; branch names are mutable, implementation must pin a release or commit','fit_and_limits':'See Chapter 4 component table; no empirical performance endorsement'})\ndocs/design/research/omissions.json:174:      \"work\": \"Merigoux et al., reverse engineering French income tax\",\ndocs/design/research/omissions.json:176:      \"action\": \"tax-language context; not corporate accounting evidence\",\ndocs/design/research/omissions.json:1015:      \"reason\": \"Neural adsorption models concern chemical mechanisms rather than corporate records or financial forecasting methods adopted here.\",\ndocs/design/research/omissions.json:1132:      \"reason\": \"Carbon-information continuity ra